libjuice 3c40a354: on macOS a dual-stack socket also owns its IPv4 port

MPL-2.0, like src/udp.c; original file notice preserved unchanged.
2026-10-16: J.J. Boyd (KG4VCF), with AI-assisted implementation via Anthropic Claude Code.

Each agent binds one IPv6 wildcard socket with IPV6_V6ONLY off, so it takes
IPv4 too. On macOS that bind succeeds on a port another socket already holds
as an IPv4-only wildcard socket, both for an explicit port and for the port
the system picks for port 0, and IPv4 datagrams to the port then go to the
other socket. One end's checks are answered and nothing reaches the other,
so a data channel never opens. Seen 2026-10-16 on a Mac where an Android
emulator (qemu) held udp4 *.52282 and the test's agent bound udp46 *.52282.

The reverse order is refused (an IPv4 bind on a port a dual-stack socket
holds fails with EADDRINUSE, with or without SO_REUSEADDR or SO_REUSEPORT),
so on __APPLE__ only, and only for an IPv6 wildcard bind, the IPv4 wildcard
port is held first, the dual-stack socket binds onto it, and the hold is
closed. A port 0 bind lets the system pick the IPv4 port and tries again,
up to 16 times, when that port is taken for IPv6. An explicit port taken for
IPv4 fails with EADDRINUSE, so a port range moves on to its next port. If no
IPv4 socket can be made, the bind is unchanged. Linux already refuses the
colliding bind with EADDRINUSE and never picks a port held for IPv4 (checked
2026-10-16 on Linux 6.12); Windows was not checked. Both are unchanged.
libjuice upstream (v1.7.4 and master on 2026-10-16) has the same src/udp.c
and no option for this.

